---
source: crates/dr-agent-tools/tests/catalog.rs
expression: table
---
asset.get [read] Read an asset's metadata and live usages in the book (`usages: false` reads the record alone).
asset.list [read] List assets in the project's built-in asset library (sorted by asset id), each with its folder, its place in it and its live usages (media modules, prose embeds and links, image fields, relations). Set `trashed: true` to include Recently Deleted.
asset.move [write] Put a file of the asset library into another folder (or the top) and/or at another place among that folder's files (manual order).
asset.rename [write] Rename a live asset in the built-in asset library.
asset.restore [write] Restore a trashed asset from Recently Deleted back into the asset library (to the top when its folder is in Recently Deleted).
asset.trash [write] Move an asset in the built-in asset library to Recently Deleted; places referencing it read as placeholders while its bytes remain.
asset_folder.create [write] Make a folder (素材夹) in the asset library, last among the folders of its parent (or the top).
asset_folder.list [read] List the folders of the asset library, in manual order within each parent (with `trashed: true`, those in Recently Deleted too).
asset_folder.move [write] Put a folder of the asset library into another folder (or the top) and/or at another place among its siblings; a folder never goes into itself or below itself.
asset_folder.rename [write] Rename a folder of the asset library.
asset_folder.restore [write] Bring a folder of the asset library back from Recently Deleted with what went there with it (to the top when its parent is still in Recently Deleted).
asset_folder.trash [write] Move a folder of the asset library to Recently Deleted with everything in it (its files and folders), in one step: places using those files read as placeholders.
book.get [read] Read book metadata and project word targets; aggregate counts use the statistics adapter.
book.sequence [read] Read the ordered book sequence: the whole project from the root, book prose only (empty when the project has no book).
book.update [write] Update selected book metadata fields without replacing other book data.
book.writing_days [read] A book's writing days: words written per day (all devices), today, the last seven days, active days and the streak.
comment.anchors [read] The stored anchors of the comments on one entity's prose, for an editor laying them out (comments without an anchor are not listed).
comment.create [write] Add a comment on an entry, any module of an entry, a sub-part inside that module, a passage of prose, or floating. Targets an entry, any module of an entry, a module sub-part (field, kv_row, plot_cell, patch, media, embed), a prose block or passage, an asset-library file (with optional region), or floating. Read the target or module first; read exact source prose before anchoring (a block needs that paragraph; ranges, anchor bytes, and embeds need the full document).
comment.list [read] List comments (a target or module sub-part in the trash or gone shows as unlocated).
comment.reanchor [write] Put a comment's anchor back where the editor's undo brought its text back (the author's change, synced; it follows the editor's undo, so it is not an entry of the object-level undo). Read the comment and its complete source prose before setting an anchor; both versions are guarded. Clearing needs the comment read.
comment.update [write] Change a comment: its text, status (resolve / reopen; a checklist item's comment ticks or clears the item in the prose) or linked Agent conversation. A checklist item's comment takes no other text: its words are the line's, so change the line in the prose. Comments have no priority. Resolving or reopening a checklist comment (origin = "task") ticks or clears the item in its source prose; read both the comment and that prose document first.
entry.create [write] Create an entry either residing under `parent` (without membership) or attached to `host` (residing in `host`'s home, `host` itself when `level`, or the root).
entry.get [read] Read an entry including retained module values; an entry whose switch is on also carries what lies below it (resolved settings and their sources, children, members).
entry.list [read] Find entries, or list the entries living under one in manuscript order; with below, each listed entry whose switch is on carries its resolved settings, children and members.
entry.move [write] Move an entry's residence and its position in the folder's manual order (`index` among the folder's stored children). Moving where an entry lives never hangs it on a lane or moves it on a timeline scale.
entry.restore [write] Restore an entry and what lives under it from trash; if its stored residence can no longer hold entries, relocate it to the nearest live level ancestor or the root.
entry.set_level [write] Turn an entry's level switch on or off. Turning it off moves all live descendants below it to the trash in place (leaving already-trashed entries, overrides, memberships and swimlanes untouched) in one commit that one undo reverts.
entry.trash [write] Trash an entry with what lives under it, keeping entries that only belong to it.
entry.update [write] Rename an entry, set its mark, switch its template or change the overrides of what lives below it, without deleting retained content or touching its switch.
entry_history.restore [write] Restore selected entry metadata (switch included) or structural records from a snapshot as a new undoable commit.
entry_history.version [read] Read the current book version for per-entry snapshot restoration.
entry_relation_type.delete [write] Delete an unused unlocked relation type.
entry_relation_type.list [read] List relation types available to entry modules.
entry_relation_type.put [write] Create or edit a relation type with endpoint and template restrictions.
entry_template.delete [write] Delete an unused template without deleting its retained prose documents.
entry_template.list [read] List the templates of a book: modules, the switch's default, linkable, settings and actions.
entry_template.put [write] Create or replace a template definition (any modules, the switch's default, linkable, settings, actions) while retaining existing entry content and switches.
entry_template.put_module [write] Add or update a module definition of any kind on a template.
entry_template.remove_module [write] Remove a module definition while retaining values and prose on existing entries.
fact.list [read] List the book's project facts (项目设定), in order.
fact.put [write] Add a project fact or change one (key, value, position).
fact.remove [write] Remove a project fact.
history.points [read] List a prose document's history points (kept 30 days, at most one every 15 minutes of writing), newest first, with the words at each.
history.preview [read] Read a prose document as it was at a history point: its paragraphs (with H1/H2/H3 levels) and its owner's title and summary then.
layout.clear [write] Clear entry coordinates (or the whole `<scope>/<mode>` view) in `layout:<book>` (not undoable; leaves the book document untouched).
layout.get [read] Read the free-canvas coordinates stored for a `<scope>/<mode>` view in `layout:<book>`.
layout.put [write] Set entry coordinates in a `<scope>/<mode>` view of `layout:<book>` (not undoable; leaves the book document untouched).
membership.add [write] Add or order a membership without moving its residence.
membership.list [read] List valid memberships in a book.
membership.primary [write] Choose an existing membership as primary, or clear the primary membership.
membership.remove [write] Remove a membership and any timeline placements left without a lane. Requires a complete timeline_view.list at the guarded version; hidden timeline data prevents removal until the kernel cascade preserves it.
memory.add [write] Add a Project Memory entry (plain text; takes effect at once).
memory.list [read] List the book's Project Memory entries.
memory.remove [write] Remove a Project Memory entry (kept as removed; restorable).
memory.restore [write] Bring back a removed Project Memory entry.
memory.update [write] Change a Project Memory entry's text, kind or status.
module.list [read] List an entry's current and retained modules and their registered prose documents.
module.put_grid [write] Replace the rows, columns, cells and size of one plot module.
module.put_kv [write] Add or update an ordered key/value row in a module.
module.put_media [write] Add or update an ordered file item in an entry's media module.
module.put_patch [write] Create or edit the text of a patch module record, preserving its source reference.
module.put_relation [write] Associate an entry with a live endpoint through its relations module.
module.read [read] Read a current or retained module and its structured content; a children module reads what lies below the entry.
module.read_prose [read] Read an entry's or a template's prose module with block IDs and version.
module.remove_kv [write] Remove a key/value row from a module.
module.remove_media [write] Remove a file item from an entry's media module.
module.remove_patch [write] Remove one patch record without deleting relations or other entries.
module.remove_relation [write] Remove one relation projected in an entry's relations module.
module.replace_prose [write] Replace an exact passage in an entry's prose module, addressed by block ID.
module.set_arrangement [write] Choose how this entry lays out the files of its media module (`featured`, `grid` or `list`), or go back to its template's (`null`); the template is not changed.
module.set_field [write] Set or clear a typed field value in an entry's fields module.
module.set_prose [write] Write paragraphs of an entry's or a template's prose module: all of them, in order, as module.read_prose reads them. Unchanged paragraphs stay; one whose words change is edited in place and keeps its id and comments. A list item has `list` (bullet, ordered or task) and `list_level`. A task item is a checklist item and also a comment whose words are the line's: `checked` ticked resolves it, cleared reopens it, and taking the line out deletes the comment.
project.get [read] Read the project definition: template source, Agent instructions, book switch and word targets, the left-sidebar tabs (「全部」 always among them), the tab the project is on, and the template it is the working copy of.
project.update [write] Update the project definition: Agent instructions, whether the project has a book, and word targets; with the project's name and profile, all in one undoable write.
project_template.get [read] Read a saved project template and its validated value.
project_template.list [read] List saved project templates in this library.
project_template.save [write] Save a project's structure and selected sample items as a project template, or a template's working copy over that template. It saves the current project. A new template needs `name`; with `replace` (a template read in this turn with project_template.list or project_template.get) it keeps that template's name and description unless they are given. `agent` is the words for the Agent the template carries: left out, the project's; null, none; given, these, in the template only (the project keeps its own). A template's working copy saves only over the template it edits, which has to be read first too; there `name`, `description` and `agent` are refused, since the template takes them from the copy's own project: change those with project.update (`name`, `summary`, `agent`).
prose.link [write] Link anchored ranges of prose documents to entities (anchors, not positions: the undo of automatic links that were taken off). Link targets may be entries or asset-library files (kind = "asset"). Read the full prose document first.
prose.read [read] Read paragraphs of a prose document (text, block id, heading level; a list item's kind and level, a checklist item's tick).
prose.replace [write] Replace one exact passage of prose with new text (it must occur exactly once, in one paragraph).
prose.restore [write] Restore a prose document to an earlier version, as a new change (history stays).
prose.shape [read] Shape of a prose document: paragraphs, sentences, the share of dialogue, its checklist, the files it embeds and the files it links to (with those in Recently Deleted).
prose.unlink [write] Take links to entities off anchored ranges of prose documents (anchors, not positions: the undo of automatic links). Link targets may be entries or asset-library files (kind = "asset"). Read the full prose document first.
recent.list [read] List this device's live pages, retaining each entry's selected module.
relation.create [write] Create a canonical relation, validating live endpoints and template restrictions; return an existing semantic edge without a write.
relation.delete [write] Delete one canonical relation without deleting either endpoint.
relation.list [read] List canonical relations whose endpoints are live, optionally scoped to an endpoint or type.
tab.move [write] Change a left-sidebar tab's position (「全部」 included).
tab.put [write] Add a left-sidebar tab that mounts a level entry or the book sequence; one thing is mounted on one tab. A tab cannot mount a view (views go with the bottom panel).
tab.remove [write] Remove a left-sidebar tab (not what it mounts); 「全部」 stays.
tab.select [write] Remember the left-sidebar tab the project is on, for every device (kept in `layout:<book>`; not undoable; leaves the book document untouched).
timeline_view.delete [write] Delete a timeline view while retaining lane entries and memberships; lanes leaving their last live view save the scale order of what is hung on them back to the memberships' manual order in the same commit.
timeline_view.list [read] List timeline views with their effective lanes, placements and time pins.
timeline_view.place [write] Place or move an entry in a timeline, optionally joining a lane in the same commit.
timeline_view.put [write] Create or edit a timeline view, including its independent lane order. Whole-view replacement requires a complete timeline_view.list at the guarded version. Hidden lanes, placements or pin data prevent replacement; use supported item commands instead.
timeline_view.put_pin [write] Add or edit a time pin, optionally bound to any live entry.
timeline_view.remove [write] Remove an entry from a timeline lane and prune positions where it has no remaining lane. Requires a complete timeline_view.list at the guarded version; hidden timeline data prevents removal until the kernel cascade preserves it.
timeline_view.remove_pin [write] Remove a time pin from a timeline view.

